## Tagging

- Use Playwright details-object tags.
- Do not put tags inside test title strings.
- Every focused UI test must be covered by `@ui`.
- Every E2E test must be covered by `@e2e`.
- Every UI and E2E test must be classified with an execution-scope tag: `@smoke` or `@regression`.
- `@e2e` replaces `@ui` as the layer tag for full-journey E2E specs by default.
- Do not tag full-journey E2E specs with `@ui` unless the project map explicitly requires both.
- Browser-based E2E may run in existing browser projects but must be tagged `@e2e` for reporting and filtering.
- Tags may be applied at `describe` level when they apply to all tests inside the suite.
- Test-level tags may add more specific classification.
- Do not introduce new tags without adding them to the tag registry or project map.

### Good

```ts
test.describe("Product sorting", { tag: ["@ui", "@regression", "@sorting"] }, () => {
  test("sorts products by price", async ({ homePage }) => {
    await test.step("Apply ascending price sorting", async () => {
      await homePage.sorting.applySort("price,asc");
    });
  });
});
```

### Good

```ts
test("shows login page for guest user", { tag: ["@ui", "@smoke", "@login"] }, async ({ loginPage }) => {
  await test.step("Open login page", async () => {
    await loginPage.open();
    await loginPage.waitForReady();
  });
});
```

### Good E2E

```ts
test(
  "completes registration and reaches authenticated account area",
  { tag: ["@e2e", "@regression", "@registration"] },
  async ({ registerPage, loginPage, accountPage }) => {
    // journey steps remain visible in the spec
  },
);
```

### Bad

```ts
test("shows login page @ui @smoke @login", async ({ loginPage }) => {
  await loginPage.open();
});
```

### Why this is bad

Tags inside title strings mix metadata with human-readable test names.

Structured tags are easier to validate, filter, and report.

---

## Tag Taxonomy

Use tags consistently by category.

### Required layer tag

Every focused UI test must have:

```text
@ui
```

Every full-journey E2E test must have:

```text
@e2e
```

Do not use both `@ui` and `@e2e` on the same full-journey E2E spec by default.

### Required execution-scope tag

By default, every UI and E2E test must have one of:

```text
@smoke
@regression
```

Quarantine exception:

- tests tagged `@wip` or `@flaky` may temporarily omit `@smoke`/`@regression`;
- default functional and focused overlay runs must exclude `@wip` and `@flaky`;
- do not keep `@wip` or `@flaky` permanently.

### Optional quality/type tags

Allowed examples:

```text
@visual
@a11y
@flaky
@wip
```

### Optional feature/domain tags

Feature/domain tags describe the product area, feature, or behavior under test.

Examples:

```text
@sorting
@filtering
@login
@register
@registration
@catalog
@contact
@checkout
@cart
@profile
@auth
```

Feature/domain tags are project-specific.

They are allowed only if defined in the tag registry or project map.

Use feature/domain tags when they add useful filtering, reporting, ownership, or traceability value.

Do not create feature/domain tags ad hoc in specs.

Do not use feature/domain tags as a replacement for required layer and execution-scope tags.

### Optional coverage-type tags for cross-browser and responsive

Use only when registered in the project map and explicitly planned in the feature or E2E journey plan.

Allowed examples when registered:

```text
@cross-browser
@responsive
```

Meaning:

- `@cross-browser` — test intent is cross-browser coverage for a documented browser-specific risk; it is **not** a browser name tag;
- `@responsive` — test intent is responsive or viewport-specific coverage for a documented layout or navigation risk; it is **not** a device or viewport size tag.

These tags classify coverage type.

They do not replace required layer tags (`@ui`, `@e2e`, `@visual`) or execution-scope tags (`@smoke`, `@regression`).

Do not use `@cross-browser` or `@responsive` on every test by default.

Use them only when the plan explicitly marks the scenario as cross-browser or responsive coverage.

### Forbidden browser, engine, and device tags

Browser, engine, viewport, and device names are execution environment details.

They are **not** Playwright test-intent tags.

Do not use tags such as:

```text
@chromium
@firefox
@webkit
@mobile
@tablet
@desktop
```

Browser and viewport selection belongs to:

- Playwright projects in `playwright.config.ts`;
- explicit viewport setup in the spec or project config when planned;
- Allure reporting metadata for reporting dimensions.

Do not encode browser or viewport in Playwright tags when a Playwright project or reporting metadata is the correct layer.

### Playwright projects vs tags

Playwright projects own browser and default viewport execution.

Tags own test intent, layer, execution scope, and optional coverage type.

Agents must use only Playwright projects documented in the project map.

Do not reference non-existing projects such as `ui-firefox` or `ui-mobile` until the project map registers them.

Current documented projects:

- `ui-chromium`
- `api`
- `e2e`

### Allure metadata for browser and viewport reporting

Allure metadata may describe reporting dimensions such as:

- browser or Playwright project;
- viewport;
- layer;
- feature;
- story;
- component;
- coverage type, such as cross-browser or responsive.

Allure metadata must not replace Playwright tags.

Playwright tags remain the source of truth for:

- test layer;
- smoke vs regression scope;
- optional coverage type when registered;
- feature/domain classification when registered.

Specs or approved reporting helpers may set Allure metadata.

Page Objects, Components, API clients, builders, and generators must not set Allure metadata.

Do not add Allure reporter integration, tokens, or credentials as part of tag policy changes.

Rule reference:

- `.cursor/rules/reporting-allure.mdc`

### Tag category examples

```ts
export const tags = {
  layer: ["@ui", "@api", "@e2e"],
  execution: ["@smoke", "@regression"],
  quality: ["@visual", "@a11y", "@flaky", "@wip"],
  coverageType: ["@cross-browser", "@responsive"],
  feature: ["@sorting", "@filtering", "@login", "@register", "@registration"],
  domain: ["@catalog", "@contact", "@checkout", "@cart", "@profile", "@auth"],
} as const;
```

These examples are illustrative.

The actual allowed tags must be documented by the project tag registry or project map.

